# music.ges — `spec/music.md`
#
# **Not part of the core prelude.**  It declares eight constructors, and a
# constructor's tag is its position, so merging this into every program
# would renumber `Nil`/`Cons` for all of them — and cost every non-musical
# program the compile time, which is superlinear in program size.  The MIDI
# backend prepends this to a music program's source instead.
#
# A `[: a :]` is a box-layout tree over a payload `a`.  `layout` resolves it
# into timed events; the payload is opaque to everything here.

#: A committed, playable event: program, key, velocity.  Concrete because
#: MIDI is the only backend; this type is the seam where another would go.
# `Midi` is a pitched note on some program; `Perc` is an unpitched one.
# The distinction is musical, not a transport detail — percussion is a
# different *kind* of sound, and which MIDI channel carries it is the
# renderer's business (`gestate/midi.py`).
Rendered := Midi Int Int Int
          | Perc Int Int

#: The other kind of committed note: one assigned to a `voices` bank of the
#: audio backend.  **Opaque here on purpose** — its constructors are
#: generated per program, one per declared bank, each carrying that bank's
#: own payload type.  This file only needs to know that `Assigned` carries
#: *something* and that it is not the payload `a`, which is what keeps
#: `[: Void :]` proving that every note was assigned.
#:
#: `Voice` and `Rendered` are the same construct at different ages: a sum
#: over the targets a backend offers, each carrying its target's payload.
#: They are kept side by side rather than merged because merging would make
#: `Rendered` — and therefore this file — partly generated, and two is not
#: yet a pattern.
#:
#: A program with no banks gets `Voice := NoVoice` from the expander, since
#: gestate cannot declare a type with no constructors at all (`fixme.md`
#: F60) and `Score` names this one.

# **`Par` and not `Over`**, which is what it was called.  `gui.ges` declares
# an `Over Sub Sub` for painter's order, and a file that plays a `score` and
# draws a `substrate` is compiled with both preludes in front of it — one
# program, two declarations of one name, and whichever came last silently
# took it.  `over a b = Over a b` in `gui.ges` then failed to type-check
# against `Score`, so the two features could not be used in one file at all;
# worse, `gui.py` reads `cons["Over"].tag` by name to walk the picture, which
# would have found the *score*'s constructor and drawn by the wrong tag.
#
# The name is internal either way — what a piece writes is `||` and what a
# canvas writes is `over` — so the rename costs no program a line.  `Seq`
# and `Par` is also the pair every other music language uses.

Score a := Note a                # `'x` — unassigned, carries the payload
         | Play Rendered         # committed to MIDI: parametric in `a`
         | Assigned Voice        # committed to a `voices` bank; likewise
         | Rest
         | Seq (Score a) (Score a)
         | Par (Score a) (Score a)   # `||` — at the same time
         | At Int (Score a)
         | Scale (Score a) Int
         | Shrink (Score a) Int
         | Retro (Score a)          # `reverse` — back to front
         | Sow Int (Score a)         # `sow n s` — re-root a subtree's seed
         | Clip Int (Score a)        # `long n s` — a declared span
         | Mark String               # a named point, zero wide
         # Ariadne (`spec/ariadne.md`): the reactive surface as lawful
         # leaves of the algebra, free-monad shaped — a value arrives, the
         # continuation is the rest of the subtree, and no box clips it.
         # `Draw` is eliminated by the sower (a draw is pure given seed
         # and position); `Hear` reaches the performer as a question whose
         # answer places what follows.
         | Draw (Int -> Score a)
         # A **channel** the score listens to, its stamped position
         # key, and what the reading decides.  The channel is a `Chan`
         # and not an integer alias: `Chan` is a builtin type in scope
         # here, and `NChan` already carries a unique id, so the alias
         # `Port = Int` this replaced was a fiction standing where a
         # type belonged (`spec/shape.md`).
         | Hear (Chan (List Int)) Int (List Int -> Score a)
         | TempoShape (List Tempo) (Score a)
         # A channel the score writes across a span, and a point at
         # which it waits for one (`spec/shape.md`).  A fermata's
         # channel is `Chan Int` — what it reads is *hold while
         # non-zero*, which a pedal, a knob and a held-key count all
         # spell the same way.
         | Shape (Chan Float) (List Envelope) (Score a)
         | Fermata (Chan Int)

#: **Ticks to the beat.**  96 divides by 2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,32,48, so every
#: division anyone writes is exact — and the whole score is laid out in
#: these, so a bar of four is `4 * ticksPerBeat`.
#:
#: It was `beat`, and the plain word has gone to the *audio* side, where
#: `beat : Sig Float` is what time it is in beats.  Two things called
#: "beat" is one too many, and of the two this is the one that is really a
#: resolution rather than a beat.
ticksPerBeat : Int
ticksPerBeat = 96

r : [: a :]
r = Rest

# `++` is *music sequence* here, shadowing the core prelude's list append.
# A class with two instances would be the general answer and is not needed:
# An *instance* rather than a redefinition, which is what stops it hiding
# the prelude's list append — see `prelude.ges`, where `++` is now
# `Semigroup`'s method.  A music program has both again.
instance Semigroup (Score b) where
    (++) s t = Seq s t

(||) : [: a :] -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
(||) a b = Par a b

#: **Retrograde** — the phrase backwards, which is the third of the four
#: things a canon does to a subject.
#:
#: A *constructor* rather than a walk that turns the tree inside out, and
#: `||` is why: an overlay is **left-aligned**, so reversing `a || b`
#: structurally would leave two voices of unequal length still starting
#: together, when what a retrograde means is that they still *end*
#: together.  The duration is not known until the subtree is laid out, so
#: the mirror happens where the duration is — in `layOnto`, against the
#: events, exactly as `Scale` does.
#:
#: `reverse` and not `retrograde`, because that is what this operation is
#: called everywhere else it appears; `prelude.ges` has the class and the
#: list instance.
instance Reversible Score where
    reverse s = Retro s

# `at` translates a score's *extent* and leaves its *duration* alone, which
# is why `a ++ at (-1) b` overlaps `a` without moving what follows.
at : Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
at n s = At n s

(|<) : [: a :] -> [: a :]
(|<) s = At (0 - ticksPerBeat) s

(>|) : [: a :] -> [: a :]
(>|) s = At ticksPerBeat s

(|*) : [: a :] -> Int -> [: a :]
(|*) s k = Scale s k

(|/) : [: a :] -> Int -> [: a :]
(|/) s k = Shrink s k

# Substitute a score for each *unassigned* note.  `Play` is left alone —
# it carries no payload — which is why "ignored if an instrument is already
# selected" needs no runtime mark.
# An *instance*, for the reason `++` above is one.  A score's `pure` is
# `Note`, which is exactly what `'x` already writes.
instance Monad Score where
    pure x = Note x

    (>>=) s f = case s of
        Note x -> f x
        Play e -> Play e
        # Committed, so an instrument does not reach it — the same reason
        # `Play` is left alone, and what makes `>>=` idempotent on a bound
        # score.
        Assigned v -> Assigned v
        Rest -> Rest
        Seq a b -> Seq (a >>= f) (b >>= f)
        Par a b -> Par (a >>= f) (b >>= f)
        At n t -> At n (t >>= f)
        Scale t k -> Scale (t >>= f) k
        Shrink t k -> Shrink (t >>= f) k
        Retro t -> Retro (t >>= f)
        # The instrument reaches *through* a decision not yet made: bind
        # composes with the continuation, so `sown g >>= voices.lead`
        # assigns whatever the seed decides.
        Sow n t -> Sow n (t >>= f)
        Clip w t -> Clip w (t >>= f)
        Mark n -> Mark n
        Draw g -> Draw (sd => g sd >>= f)
        Hear p ky g -> Hear p ky (xs => g xs >>= f)
        TempoShape e u -> TempoShape e (u >>= f)
        Shape c e u -> Shape c e (u >>= f)
        Fermata c -> Fermata c

#: **A MIDI program number, as an instrument.**  `melody >>= prog 73`
#: commits every unassigned note in `melody` to program 73 — a flute.
#:
#: An instrument is any function from a note's payload to a committed
#: score, applied with `>>=`; this is the one that names a MIDI program,
#: and `percussion` is the other.  A score bound with one holds no payload,
#: so it is parametric and unifies with `Void` — which is what makes it
#: performable.
#:
#: Named `prog` and not `instrument` because a composer wants that word:
#: this file is prepended as *text*, so a piece that defines its own
#: `instrument` would have collided with the library rather than shadowed
#: it, and "instrument" is the first thing anyone reaches for when naming
#: the four voices of a quartet.
prog : Int -> Int -> [: b :]
prog n pitch = Play (Midi n pitch 64)

#: The percussion instrument.  It needs no program number, so it is itself
#: the function `>>=` wants: `pattern >>= percussion`.
percussion : Int -> [: b :]
percussion key = Play (Perc key 96)

lay : [: a :] -> (Int, List (Int, Int, Rendered))
lay s = case layOnto 0 s [] of
    (d, es) -> (d, reverse es)

layout : [: Void :] -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
layout s = snd (lay s)

#: The audio backend's `layout`: a piece as bank-assigned, timed events.
layVoices : [: Void :] -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
layVoices s = reverse (snd (layVoicesOnto 0 s []))

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── Tempo ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **A piece's tempo, as an envelope over beats.**  A piece writes either a
# plain `bpm : Int`, which is what it always wrote, or
#
#     tempo : List Tempo
#     tempo = [Step 0.0 120.0, Ramp 32.0 90.0, Step 48.0 120.0]
#
# and never both — two answers to how fast a piece goes is not a question
# this can settle, so it is refused by name.
#
# **Linear in *time*, not in beat, and that is the whole design.**  The two
# readings look interchangeable and are not: with tempo linear in time,
# beat is its integral — a quadratic — and a quadratic inverts in closed
# form, so "when does beat 48 happen" is the quadratic formula and costs
# nothing.  That inverse is not elegance, it is four features: a schedule
# precomputed into sample instants, seeking to a bar, a bar ruler, and
# looping between bars.  An arbitrary `Sig Float` tempo has none of them —
# it can only be integrated forward from zero, so a seek is a replay.
#
# `Step b y` steps to `y` at beat `b`, holding the previous tempo right up
# to it.  `Ramp b y` slides to `y`, arriving exactly at beat `b`.  The flag
# is a property of the point being *arrived at*, so "get to 90 by bar 9"
# says nothing about what happens after bar 9.

#: **A tempo *is* an envelope** — of beats per minute, against beats — so
#: this is `audio.ges`'s `Envelope` under the name a piece calls it by,
#: rather than a second type with the same two constructors.  Declaring it
#: twice would give a scored program two `Step`s, and a constructor's name
#: is the whole program's.
type Tempo = Envelope

# ── Forever is a value — `spec/dynamicscore.md`, stage two ───────────────

#: `cycle bar` is `bar`, then `cycle bar` — which the lazy machine only
#: ever builds as far as somebody plays it.  Nothing here is new to the
#: language; what is new is a performer that stops demanding.
cycle : [: a :] -> [: a :]
cycle s = s ++ cycle s

#: A generative score: `unfold seed next`, where `next` grows one section
#: and the state the following one grows from.  Seed it and it is
#: replayable — the transcript records the seed, not the surprise.
unfold : b -> (b -> ([: a :], b)) -> [: a :]
unfold z f = case f z of
    (s, z2) -> s ++ unfold z2 f

#: The stream a dynamic performance forces — `layVoices`' twin, an event
#: list in **beat order, lazy in its tail**, so a performer can force
#: enough to cover the next few beats and no more.  Like `layVoices` it
#: is named by a generated entry point (`streamMain`), which is why it
#: stays above the marker; the walk it fronts is below.
streamVoices : [: Void :] -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
streamVoices s = streamVoicesAt 0 s

#: The stream a *listening* performance forces — `streamVoices` with one
#: more power: it can end in a question.  A `CueAsk` is a `hear` reached:
#: its continuation holds **the entire rest of the performance**, so the
#: host answers it with the port's reading at the downbeat and simply
#: keeps walking the list the answer returns.  No rebuilding, no second
#: walk — the question is the spine, suspended.
liveVoices : [: Void :] -> List Cue
liveVoices s = liveTo 0 s

# ── Reading the seed — `spec/dynamicscore.md`, stage three ───────────────
#
# The seed rides the layout tree: `++` and `||` each split it, left and
# right, so every subtree owns a stream of randomness derived from its
# *place in the piece*.  A draw at bar 33 is a descent's worth of splits;
# two draws under one overlay are independent because they sit on
# different split paths; each turn of a `cycle` is a fresh split.  The
# arithmetic is SplitMix64-shaped and lives below the marker — the
# fragment's own noise (`white`, `dust`) keeps its LCG, because that one
# must fit an i64 for the compiled code and the interpreter to agree.
# The LCG is a texture; this is an index.

#: Re-root a subtree's seed: `sow 7 intro ++ improvisation` is fixed
#: art inside an improvisation.
sow : Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
sow n s = Sow n s

#: **A declared span: this branch is `n` beats long, whatever its
#: content does.**  `long 16 verse` pins the verse to sixteen beats —
#: content past the box is clipped, content short of it rests out the
#: difference — which is the stage-three invariant handed to the
#: author: content bends, time doesn't.
#:
#: The declaration pays twice.  Today it is structure a reader can
#: trust; next it is what lets a performer *skip*: a branch whose width
#: is written down can be passed over without forcing what is inside,
#: so resuming a long-running piece descends by arithmetic instead of
#: unfolding from the left.  (A listening bar wants one: a question's
#: own width is unknown until it is answered, so `long 1 (do ks <-
#: hear p; …)` is what lets a rebuild skip past the bar it stands
#: in — see `opaqueHead`.)
long : Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
long n s = Clip (n * ticksPerBeat) s

#: **A bar point** — zero beats wide, so `mark 1 ++ verse` moves
#: nothing.  A mark is a place the music can be re-entered: the
#: performer enumerates them (`streamMarks`, below with the walks)
#: without touching a single note, and a resume names one instead of
#: unfolding everything to its left.  Numbered rather than named,
#: the way seeds are: what matters is that the same one means the
#: same place twice.
mark : String -> [: a :]
mark n = Mark n

#: **A named span** — `section "verse" body` marks where the verse
#: begins and plays it.  A *point*, not a wrapper: the mark is zero
#: wide and the body follows it, so a section costs the algebra
#: nothing and `durOf (section n s)` is `durOf s` by construction.
#:
#: The idiom this exists for is Henri's — the form of a piece written
#: as *payloads* and bound to the function that plays them:
#:
#:     score = ('"opening" ++ '"verse" ++ '"closing") >>= scoreParts
#:
#:     scoreParts name = section name (case name of
#:         "opening" -> intro
#:         "verse" -> verse
#:         _ -> coda)
#:
#: Bind preserves the spine exactly, so a position in the form is a
#: position in the music: that is what makes these names *paths*
#: rather than decorations, and what lets a transport re-enter by one
#: (`streamMarks`, `spec/ariadne.md`).
section : String -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
section name s = Seq (Mark name) s

#: **A tempo, written where it happens.**  `tempoShape` bends the
#: clock across one subtree's span — a ritardando in the bar it
#: belongs to, rather than at the top of the file:
#:
#:     coda = tempoShape [Step 0.0 120.0, Ramp 1.0 60.0] lastBar
#:
#: The points are the same `Tempo` a piece writes globally — absolute
#: beats-per-minute — and their beats are **fractions of the span**,
#: so the shape scales with `|*` and reverses with `reverse` the way
#: any annotation over a subtree does.
#:
#: **It is spliced into the piece's one envelope, not integrated.**
#: `tempo.py` keeps tempo linear in *time* precisely so beat inverts
#: in closed form, and that inverse is four features (a precomputed
#: schedule, seek-to-bar, the bar ruler, looping).  A local shape is
#: therefore a *rewrite* of the global envelope at two computable
#: beats — one envelope still, still invertible — and never a second
#: clock integrated forward, which is the shape `music.ges` refused
#: when it chose an envelope over a `Sig Float` in the first place.
tempoShape : List Tempo -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
tempoShape e s = TempoShape e s

#: **A channel written across a span** — a crescendo said where it
#: happens.  The points' beats are *fractions of the span*, so the
#: shape scales with `|*` and reverses with `reverse` as any
#: annotation over a subtree does, and a flat envelope is a knob set
#: once.  What it writes is automation at control rate, which in
#: gestate is once per block; smoothing finer than that is `slew`'s
#: job in signal land.
shape : Chan Float -> List Envelope -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
shape c e s = Shape c e s

#: **Wait here until the world says go.**  Zero beats wide, like a
#: mark: what it holds is the *clock*, so the notes already sounding
#: simply go on sounding — their releases sit at ticks the clock has
#: not reached — and nothing in the layout moves.  It holds while the
#: channel reads non-zero and releases when it reads zero, so a pedal,
#: a knob and a count of held keys all drive it the same way.
#:
#: It holds for as long as it is held.  There is no ceiling: a fermata
#: means *wait*, the player is right there, and a transport can always
#: seek.
fermata : Chan Int -> [: a :]
fermata c = Fermata c

#: **An anonymous bar point** — `bar ++ verse ++ bar ++ chorus` marks
#: the seams without naming them.  Anonymity works because a resume
#: goes to the nearest point by *tick*, never by number; the
#: enumeration numbers these left to right for a human to point at,
#: which shifts when marks are added — a place that must keep its
#: name across edits writes `mark n` instead.
bar : [: a :]
bar = Mark ""

#: **Which reading a `hear` asks for.**  An alias of `Int` for the
#: reason `Seed` is over in `synth.ges`: the number is an identity the
#: expander assigns (`holds.<bank>` is port `k` for the `k`-th bank),
#: and what the name buys is a signature that says which integer is
#: wanted.
type Port = Int

#: **The seed at this position** — ariadne's chance leaf
#: (`spec/ariadne.md`).  A value, not a span: `do s <- draw; f s` is
#: `f` of the seed this position carries, to the tick — the sower
#: eliminates every `Draw` before any walk measures, so the algebra
#: never notices the draw happened.  N draws are N binds; a walk seeds
#: itself with `do s <- draw; unfold (mix64 s) step`.  A seed is spent,
#: not read: the usual next line is `below`, one bounded pick per draw.
draw : [: Int :]
draw = Draw (sd => 'sd)

#: **The world at this instant** — ariadne's listening leaf.  The
#: reading arrives at the leaf's own downbeat, the content it chooses
#: carries its own time, and **what follows is placed after whatever
#: the answer chose** — the one place time is decided by the thread
#: rather than the text (`spec/ariadne.md`, B7 restated).  Unlike
#: the surface this replaced, there is no box: a four-beat answer
#: takes four beats.
hear : Chan (List Int) -> [: List Int :]
hear c = Hear c 0 (xs => 'xs)

#: Every mark and where it falls, `(tick, mark)`, beat-ordered and
#: lazy — `streamVoices`' little sibling, named by its own generated
#: entry (`marksMain`), which is why it stands above the marker.
#: Every `tempoShape` and the span it covers, `(from, to, points)` in
#: ticks — the piece's own tempo rewrites, enumerated the way marks
#: are and for the same reason: a host has to know them *before* it
#: can schedule, and an endless piece must be able to answer for a
#: prefix.
tempoSpans : [: Void :] -> List (Int, Int, List Tempo)
tempoSpans s = tempoSpansAt 0 s

#: Every `shape` and the span it covers, `(from, to, chan, points)` in
#: ticks — read the way `tempoSpans` is, and for the same reason: the
#: host writes the channel and must know where the span is before it
#: can.
shapeSpans : [: Void :] -> List (Int, Int, Chan Float, List Envelope)
shapeSpans s = shapeSpansAt 0 s

streamMarks : [: Void :] -> List (Int, String)
streamMarks s = streamMarksAt 0 s

#: **The remainder of a score from tick `t` on** — what a resume plays,
#: with `t` rebased to zero.  The walk *descends by declared widths*:
#: a `Clip` whose box ends before `t` is skipped without forcing one
#: thing inside it, which is what turns resuming a long-running piece
#: from an unfolding into an arithmetic.  Run on the **sown** tree
#: (`resumeMain` composes it after `sowScore`), so every surviving
#: subtree keeps exactly the seed its position always had.
#:
#: Two honest slops, both bounded by where resumes happen (beat and
#: bar points): a cut inside `Scale k` floors to the nearest multiple
#: of `k`, and a cut *into* a retrograde keeps it whole — a mirror has
#: no remainder that is not also a different piece.
resumeAt : Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
resumeAt t s = case t < 1 of
    True -> s
    False -> case s of
        Seq a b -> resumeSeq t (durOf a) a b
        Par a b -> Par (resumeAt t a) (resumeAt t b)
        # Dropping the `At` is the rebasing: events sat at `n + x`, the
        # remainder wants `n + x - t`, and `resumeAt (t - n)` inside
        # yields exactly that.  The parent `Seq`'s `Clip` (below) keeps
        # the sibling arithmetic right where the duration shifted.
        At n u -> resumeAt (t - n) u
        Scale u k -> Scale (resumeAt (prim_div_int t k) u) k
        Shrink u k -> Shrink (resumeAt (t * k) u) k
        Retro u -> Retro u
        Clip w u -> case w <= t of
            True -> r
            False -> Clip (w - t) (resumeAt t u)
        Sow n u -> resumeAt t (sowScore n u)
        # A leaf `t` lands inside is a note already begun: passed, and
        # dropped — the parent's `Clip` rests out what remained of it.
        Note x -> r
        Play e -> r
        Assigned v -> r
        Rest -> r
        Mark n -> r
        # Ariadne's leaves distribute: the cut applies to whatever the
        # value decides, when it is decided — and the thread answers a
        # rejoined question by its stamped key, so a resume lands on the
        # answers the take gave (`spec/ariadne.md`, the thread).
        #
        # **A joint of undeclared width cannot be skipped**: `durOf` of
        # an unanswered question is 0, so the `Seq` arithmetic has
        # nothing to step over.  `resumeSeq` stops there rather than
        # stepping repeatedly (which advanced nothing and fell silent),
        # so the phrase **restarts at the joint** — defined, audible,
        # and answered from the thread by key.  Declaring the width is
        # the cure and the idiom: `cycle (long 1 (do ks <- hear p; …))`
        # skips by arithmetic like anything else.
        Draw g -> Draw (sd => resumeAt t (g sd))
        Hear p ky g -> Hear p ky (xs => resumeAt t (g xs))
        TempoShape e u -> TempoShape e (resumeAt t u)
        Shape c e u -> Shape c e (resumeAt t u)
        Fermata c -> r

#: **Does this subtree's head hold an unanswered question?**  The one
#: thing a resume cannot step over: `durOf` of a `Hear` is 0, so the
#: `Seq` arithmetic below would drop the branch and try the next —
#: forever, on a `cycle`.  Asking first turns that into a defined
#: answer (resume *at* the joint) instead of a walk that never
#: advances.
#:
#: It terminates where the naive walk does not, and for the reason
#: worth writing down: it descends into a right sibling **only** when
#: the left is both measured-as-zero and not itself a question — a
#: chain of `mark`s, which is finite in any piece — and a declared
#: `long` answers `False` at once, because a box's width is known
#: whatever its content does.  That is the idiom this predicate exists
#: to reward.
opaqueHead : [: a :] -> Bool
opaqueHead s = case s of
    Hear p ky g -> True
    Draw g -> True
    Seq a b -> case opaqueHead a of
        True -> True
        False -> case durOf a < 1 of
            True -> opaqueHead b
            False -> False
    Par a b -> case opaqueHead a of
        True -> True
        False -> opaqueHead b
    At n u -> opaqueHead u
    Scale u k -> opaqueHead u
    Shrink u k -> opaqueHead u
    Sow n u -> opaqueHead u
    TempoShape e u -> opaqueHead u
    Shape c e u -> opaqueHead u
    Fermata c -> False
    _ -> False

resumeSeq : Int -> Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
resumeSeq t da a b = case t < da of
    # The cut branch is boxed to its shortened width, or every sibling
    # after it would land early by exactly the material removed.
    True -> Seq (Clip (da - t) (resumeAt t a)) b
    # **Stop where the arithmetic runs out.**  A zero-measured branch
    # holding an unanswered question is not empty — it is unmeasured —
    # so stepping over it would drop a bar that will play, and stepping
    # over it *repeatedly* is the walk that never advances.  Resuming
    # here instead is defined and audible: the phrase restarts at the
    # joint rather than the piece falling silent, and declaring the
    # width (`long n`) skips it properly.
    False -> case da < 1 of
        True -> case opaqueHead a of
            True -> Seq a b
            False -> resumeAt (t - da) b
        False -> resumeAt (t - da) b

#: One value, drawn whole from a seed — the class is what lets an
#: author's payload be drawn the way it is built, one split per field.
#: An instance receives a seed it owns outright; what it does not
#: `split` it must not reuse.
class Random a where
    random : Int -> a

instance Random Float where
    random s = unit s

instance Random Bool where
    random s = unit s < 0.5

#: Two independent seeds from one — how randomness travels *into*
#: ordinary code: thread one half, hand on the other.
split : Int -> (Int, Int)
split s = (mix64 (wrap64 (s + 11400714819323198485)),
           mix64 (wrap64 (s + 4354685564936845354)))

#: A seed narrowed to a **position key** that fits a signed 64-bit
#: slot.  A seed is 64 unsigned bits and the key travels through two
#: machines, one of which counts in signed integers (`crust`); one bit
#: is the whole price, and identity is what the number is for.
keyOf : Int -> Int
keyOf s = prim_mod_int s 9223372036854775807

#: **One draw in `0 .. n-1`** — the standard way a seed becomes a
#: bounded choice.  `draw`'s usual companion: `do s <- draw` hands the
#: position its seed, and `below n s` spends it on one uniform pick —
#: usually an index into a table of figures, which is `moods.ges`'s
#: whole chancy line (`long 4 (phraseOf (below 4 s))`).  Two
#: *independent* picks want two seeds (`split`, or a second `draw`);
#: two `below`s over one seed make correlated choices.
below : Int -> Int -> Int
below n s = prim_mod_int (mix64 (wrap64 s)) n

#: `draw`, with the seed already spent on one uniform draw.  The
#: content's time is its own — ariadne has no box to fit.
roll : (Float -> [: a :]) -> [: a :]
roll f = do
    s <- draw
    f (unit s)

#: Play `s` with probability `p`, else a beat of rest.  (Under the old
#: boxed surface both arms were clipped alike; ariadne keeps the time
#: of whichever arm plays, so give the arms equal width yourself when
#: `s` is wider than a beat.)
chance : Float -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
chance p s = roll (x => case x < p of
    True -> s
    False -> r)

#: **Where the seed enters the tree** — named by the generated entry
#: points beside `layVoices` and `streamVoices`, which is why it stays
#: above the marker.  A score with no `draw` in it passes through
#: unchanged whatever the seed, which is what keeps fixed art fixed.
sowScore : Int -> [: a :] -> [: a :]
sowScore sd s = case s of
    Sow n t -> sowScore n t
    Seq a b -> case split sd of
        (l, r) -> Seq (sowScore l a) (sowScore r b)
    Par a b -> case split sd of
        (l, r) -> Par (sowScore l a) (sowScore r b)
    At n t -> At n (sowScore sd t)
    Scale t k -> Scale (sowScore sd t) k
    Shrink t k -> Shrink (sowScore sd t) k
    Retro t -> Retro (sowScore sd t)
    Clip w t -> Clip w (sowScore sd t)
    Note x -> Note x
    Play e -> Play e
    Assigned v -> Assigned v
    Rest -> Rest
    Mark n -> Mark n
    # **The sower eliminates `Draw` entirely**, and no walk below this
    # line ever meets one on a sown tree — the constructor's whole tax
    # is this one arm.  The draw's value is the position's own seed —
    # the bit contract `test_sownscore.py` pins, unchanged from the old
    # surface, so takes replay across the retirement — and the
    # continuation is re-seeded with the right split, so a *chain* of
    # draws is a chain of fresh values, which the old surface could not
    # spell at all.
    Draw g -> case split sd of
        (l, r2) -> sowScore r2 (g sd)
    # **The key is the position's own seed**, which costs nothing to
    # thread because the sower already carries it — and the split is
    # what makes a *chain* of questions distinct: the question takes
    # the left, its continuation the right, exactly as `Draw` does.
    # (Two `sow`-pinned copies of one listening subtree would collide;
    # `sow` means fixed art, and a listening subtree is not that.)
    Hear p ky g -> case split sd of
        (l, r2) -> Hear p (keyOf l) (xs => sowScore r2 (g xs))
    TempoShape e u -> TempoShape e (sowScore sd u)
    Shape c e u -> Shape c e (sowScore sd u)
    Fermata c -> Fermata c

internal
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# **The layout half, and none of it is a composer's.**  A piece is written
# with the dozen names above — `'`, `r`, `++`, `||`, `at`, `|*`, `|/`,
# `prog`, `percussion`.  These turn one into a list of timed events,
# which is the backend's job and nobody else's: measured, not one of them
# is named from any `.ges` file in this repository.
#
# `lay`, `layout` and `layVoices` stay above because the generated entry
# point names them — `main = (bpm, layout score)` — so they are the seam
# between a piece and whatever plays it.
#
# The `…V` pair beside each `place…` is the same function over a `Voice`
# rather than a `Rendered`; that duplication is invisible from here, which
# is the point of the marker.

maxInt : Int -> Int -> Int
maxInt a b = case a < b of
    True -> b
    False -> a

# Scaling a subtree's events into place: `placeScaled off k es` puts local
# coordinate `x` of a scaled subtree at `off + x*k`.  Only `Scale` and `Shrink` need this —
# translation composes with the offset `layOnto` already carries, so there
# is no `shiftEvents` any more.
#
# These destructure in the lambda rather than projecting: a lambda
# parameter's type is not known at a projection site — `map`'s
# instantiation settles it later, and projection is resolved from the type
# (`fixme.md` F28) — while a pattern needs no such knowledge, which is what
# the error message says to do.
placeScaled : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
placeScaled off k es = map ((a, b, x) => (off + a * k, off + b * k, x)) es

placeShrunk : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
placeShrunk off k es = map ((a, b, x) => (off + prim_div_int a k, off + prim_div_int b k, x)) es

#: Mirroring a subtree's events inside its own duration — `Retro`'s half of
#: the same idea `placeScaled` is `Scale`'s.
placeRetro : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
placeRetro off dt es = map ((a, b, x) => (off + dt - b, off + dt - a, x)) es

# `(duration, events)` — the two measurements kept apart.
#
# Written the direct way, `Seq a b` is `append ea (shiftEvents da eb)`, and
# a melody is one long `Seq` chain: each level copies the events gathered so
# far, so laying out n notes costs O(n²).  Nesting the chain the other way
# only moves the cost from `append` to `shiftEvents`.
#
# `layOnto` removes both.  It carries the offset *down* instead of shifting
# events up, and prepends onto an accumulator instead of appending — so it
# touches each event once.  The accumulator therefore comes out reversed,
# which `lay` undoes once at the end rather than n times on the way.
#
#     layOnto off s acc  =  (duration of s, reverse (events of s at off) ++ acc)

layOnto : Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> (Int, List (Int, Int, Rendered))
layOnto off s acc = case s of
    Note x -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    Play e -> (ticksPerBeat, (off, off + ticksPerBeat, e) :: acc)
    # Laid out by `layVoices`, not here: this function's events are
    # `Rendered`, and a bank-assigned note is not one.  It still takes its
    # beat, so a piece keeps its shape whichever backend reads it.
    Assigned v -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    Rest -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    # `a` first, then `b` at `off + da`: prepending reverses, so laying them
    # in order leaves them in order once reversed.
    Seq a b -> case layOnto off a acc of
        (da, acc1) -> case layOnto (off + da) b acc1 of
            (db, acc2) -> (da + db, acc2)
    Par a b -> case layOnto off a acc of
        (da, acc1) -> case layOnto off b acc1 of
            (db, acc2) -> (maxInt da db, acc2)
    # A translation composes with the offset, so it stays free.
    At n t -> layOnto (off + n) t acc
    # Scaling does not: an event of `t` at local `x` belongs at `off + x*k`,
    # which is not `(off + x)*k`.  So these two lay their subtree at the
    # origin and transform its events — the one place a pass over the
    # subtree's events remains, exactly as it was before.
    Scale t k -> case layOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (dt * k, placeScaled off k et ++ acc)
    Shrink t k -> case layOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (prim_div_int dt k, placeShrunk off k et ++ acc)
    # Retrograde is the same shape: lay the subtree at the origin so its
    # own duration is known, then mirror its events inside that duration.
    # An event at `a .. b` of a subtree lasting `dt` belongs at
    # `dt - b .. dt - a`, which is what "still ends together" means.
    Retro t -> case layOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (dt, placeRetro off dt et ++ acc)
    # An un-sown decision is a rest that keeps its beat: the entry points
    # sow the tree (`sowScore`), so meeting a bare `Sown` here means a
    # path that did not, and silence with the right duration is the
    # honest sound of no decision.
    Sow n t -> layOnto off (sowScore n t) acc
    Mark n -> (0, acc)
    # An unsown draw and an unheard question are nothing at all — no
    # width, no sound: the leaves are zero-wide by law (`spec/ariadne.md`)
    # and an eager walk has no seed and no world to widen them with.
    Draw g -> (0, acc)
    Hear p ky g -> (0, acc)
    TempoShape e u -> layOnto off u acc
    Shape c e u -> layOnto off u acc
    Fermata c -> (0, acc)
    # A clipped box: lay at the origin like `Scale`, keep what starts
    # inside the window, and the box's own width is the duration —
    # content bends, time doesn't.
    Clip w t -> case layOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (w, placeFrom off (keepBelow w et) ++ acc)

# ── The other backend's half ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The same walk, collecting `Assigned` instead of `Play`.  Two functions
# rather than one parameterised by which leaf to keep: the fragment has no
# higher-order values, and a `Score` is walked by the *interpreter* anyway,
# so the duplication costs a reader nothing and buys both of them a type.

layVoicesOnto : Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> (Int, List (Int, Int, Voice))
layVoicesOnto off s acc = case s of
    Note x -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    Play e -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    Assigned v -> (ticksPerBeat, (off, off + ticksPerBeat, v) :: acc)
    Rest -> (ticksPerBeat, acc)
    Seq a b -> case layVoicesOnto off a acc of
        (da, acc1) -> case layVoicesOnto (off + da) b acc1 of
            (db, acc2) -> (da + db, acc2)
    Par a b -> case layVoicesOnto off a acc of
        (da, acc1) -> case layVoicesOnto off b acc1 of
            (db, acc2) -> (maxInt da db, acc2)
    At n t -> layVoicesOnto (off + n) t acc
    Scale t k -> case layVoicesOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (dt * k, placeScaledV off k et ++ acc)
    Shrink t k -> case layVoicesOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (prim_div_int dt k, placeShrunkV off k et ++ acc)
    Retro t -> case layVoicesOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (dt, placeRetroV off dt et ++ acc)
    # The three stage-three arms, as in `layOnto` above and for the same
    # reasons.
    Sow n t -> layVoicesOnto off (sowScore n t) acc
    Mark n -> (0, acc)
    Draw g -> (0, acc)
    Hear p ky g -> (0, acc)
    TempoShape e u -> layVoicesOnto off u acc
    Shape c e u -> layVoicesOnto off u acc
    Fermata c -> (0, acc)
    Clip w t -> case layVoicesOnto 0 t [] of
        (dt, et) -> (w, placeFromV off (keepBelowV w et) ++ acc)

placeScaledV : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
placeScaledV off k es = map (e => case e of
    (a, b, v) -> (off + a * k, off + b * k, v)) es

placeShrunkV : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
placeShrunkV off k es = map (e => case e of
    (a, b, v) -> (off + prim_div_int a k, off + prim_div_int b k, v)) es

placeRetroV : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
placeRetroV off dt es = map (e => case e of
    (a, b, v) -> (off + dt - b, off + dt - a, v)) es

# ── Clipping — the stage-three box, in both payloads ─────────────────────
#
# `keepBelow` filters, for the eager walks whose lists are in tree order;
# `takeBelowV` stops at the first event past the window, which is only
# correct on the *ordered* stream — and is what lets an endless
# continuation live inside a finite box.  A note that starts inside the
# window plays whole: a section may ring past its box, it may not begin
# past it.

placeFrom : Int -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
placeFrom off es = map (e => case e of
    (a, b, x) -> (off + a, off + b, x)) es

keepBelow : Int -> List (Int, Int, Rendered) -> List (Int, Int, Rendered)
keepBelow w es = filter (e => case e of (a, b, x) -> a < w) es

placeFromV : Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
placeFromV off es = map (e => case e of
    (a, b, v) -> (off + a, off + b, v)) es

keepBelowV : Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
keepBelowV w es = filter (e => case e of (a, b, v) -> a < w) es

takeBelowV : Int -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
takeBelowV w es = case es of
    Nil -> Nil
    e :: rest -> case e of
        (a, b, v) -> case a < w of
            True -> e :: takeBelowV w rest
            False -> Nil

# ── The marks walk — `streamVoicesAt`'s little sibling ───────────────────
#
# The same order discipline over `(tick, mark)` pairs, so a transport can
# enumerate re-entry points lazily without forcing one note: nothing here
# touches a payload, and a `Clip` bounds its branch the same way, which
# is what keeps the walk cheap on an endless piece.

mergeMarks : List (Int, String) -> List (Int, String)
          -> List (Int, String)
mergeMarks xs ys = case xs of
    Nil -> ys
    x :: xt -> case ys of
        Nil -> xs
        y :: yt -> case x of
            (a, m) -> case y of
                (c, k) -> case a <= c of
                    True -> x :: mergeMarks xt ys
                    False -> y :: mergeMarks xs yt

placeMarks : Int -> List (Int, String) -> List (Int, String)
placeMarks off ms = map (e => case e of
    (a, m) -> (off + a, m)) ms

minInt : Int -> Int -> Int
minInt a b = case a < b of
    True -> a
    False -> b

tempoSpansAt : Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, Int, List Tempo)
tempoSpansAt off s = case s of
    TempoShape e u -> (off, off + durOf u, e) :: tempoSpansAt off u
    Shape c e u -> tempoSpansAt off u
    Fermata c -> Nil
    Seq a b -> tempoSpansAt off a ++ tempoSpansAt (off + durOf a) b
    Par a b -> tempoSpansAt off a ++ tempoSpansAt off b
    At n t -> tempoSpansAt (off + n) t
    Scale t k -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, ps) -> (off + (a - off) * k, off + (b - off) * k, ps))
        (tempoSpansAt off t)
    Shrink t k -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, ps) -> (off + prim_div_int (a - off) k,
                       off + prim_div_int (b - off) k, ps))
        (tempoSpansAt off t)
    Retro t -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, ps) -> (off + durOf t - b, off + durOf t - a, flipEnv ps))
        (tempoSpansAt 0 t)
    Sow n t -> tempoSpansAt off (sowScore n t)
    Clip w t -> tempoSpansAt off t
    Mark n -> Nil
    Note x -> Nil
    Play e -> Nil
    Assigned v -> Nil
    Rest -> Nil
    Draw g -> Nil
    Hear p ky g -> Nil

#: An envelope read backwards: each point's fraction mirrored and the
#: list turned round, so a shape under `reverse` runs backwards over
#: the reversed content — the law `spec/shape.md` states.
#:
#: **The flags ride their own points**, which is the one thing a
#: mirror cannot make exact: `Step` and `Ramp` say what happens on
#: *arrival*, and arrival changes ends when time does.  A ramp
#: reversed is a ramp to where it came from, which is what a player
#: means by it; a step reversed steps at the mirrored place.
flipEnv : List Envelope -> List Envelope
flipEnv ps = shiftFlags (reverse (map flipPoint ps))

flipPoint : Envelope -> Envelope
flipPoint p = case p of
    Step b y -> Step (1.0 - b) y
    Ramp b y -> Ramp (1.0 - b) y

#: **The flags move one place along.**  `Step`/`Ramp` is a property of
#: the point being *arrived at*, so a segment's slope belongs to its
#: later end — and reversing time swaps which end that is.  Mirroring
#: the beats alone left every ramp behind a step, which reads as a
#: flat line: the whole shape went quiet while looking correct.
shiftFlags : List Envelope -> List Envelope
shiftFlags ps = case ps of
    [] -> []
    (p :: rest) -> Step (envAt p) (envVal p) :: shiftGo (envRamps p) rest

shiftGo : Bool -> List Envelope -> List Envelope
shiftGo prev ps = case ps of
    [] -> []
    (p :: rest) -> case prev of
        True -> Ramp (envAt p) (envVal p) :: shiftGo (envRamps p) rest
        False -> Step (envAt p) (envVal p) :: shiftGo (envRamps p) rest

shapeSpansAt : Int -> [: a :]
            -> List (Int, Int, Chan Float, List Envelope)
shapeSpansAt off s = case s of
    Shape c e u -> (off, off + durOf u, c, e) :: shapeSpansAt off u
    Seq a b -> shapeSpansAt off a ++ shapeSpansAt (off + durOf a) b
    Par a b -> shapeSpansAt off a ++ shapeSpansAt off b
    At n t -> shapeSpansAt (off + n) t
    Scale t k -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, c, ps) -> (off + (a - off) * k, off + (b - off) * k, c, ps))
        (shapeSpansAt off t)
    Shrink t k -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, c, ps) -> (off + prim_div_int (a - off) k,
                          off + prim_div_int (b - off) k, c, ps))
        (shapeSpansAt off t)
    # A retrograde mirrors what is inside it, shapes included: the
    # span turns round within the subtree's own width and its envelope
    # is read from the other end.
    Retro t -> map (e => case e of
        (a, b, c, ps) -> (off + durOf t - b, off + durOf t - a, c,
                          flipEnv ps))
        (shapeSpansAt 0 t)
    Sow n t -> shapeSpansAt off (sowScore n t)
    Clip w t -> shapeSpansAt off t
    TempoShape e u -> shapeSpansAt off u
    Mark n -> Nil
    Note x -> Nil
    Play e -> Nil
    Assigned v -> Nil
    Rest -> Nil
    Draw g -> Nil
    Hear p ky g -> Nil
    Fermata c -> Nil

streamMarksAt : Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, String)
streamMarksAt off s = case s of
    Mark n -> (off, n) :: Nil
    Draw g -> Nil
    Hear p ky g -> Nil
    TempoShape e u -> streamMarksAt off u
    Shape c e u -> streamMarksAt off u
    Fermata c -> Nil
    Note x -> Nil
    Play e -> Nil
    Assigned v -> Nil
    Rest -> Nil
    Seq a b -> streamMarksAt off a ++ streamMarksAt (off + durOf a) b
    Par a b -> mergeMarks (streamMarksAt off a) (streamMarksAt off b)
    At n t -> streamMarksAt (off + n) t
    Scale t k -> placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
        (a, m) -> (a * k, m)) (streamMarksAt 0 t))
    Shrink t k -> placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
        (a, m) -> (prim_div_int a k, m)) (streamMarksAt 0 t))
    Retro t -> reverse (placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
        (a, m) -> (durOf t - a, m)) (streamMarksAt 0 t)))
    Sow n t -> streamMarksAt off (sowScore n t)
    Clip w t -> placeMarks off (streamMarksTo w 0 t)

# The bounded walk a `Clip` needs — and a *filter* cannot be: a markless
# endless branch never yields the element the filter is waiting on, so
# the bound has to ride the spine and cut it where the offset crosses
# the box.  The duplication beside `streamMarksAt` is the `…V` pattern's
# again: the same walk with one more fact in hand.
streamMarksTo : Int -> Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, String)
streamMarksTo limit off s = case limit <= off of
    True -> Nil
    False -> case s of
        Mark n -> (off, n) :: Nil
        Draw g -> Nil
        Hear p ky g -> Nil
        TempoShape e u -> streamMarksTo limit off u
        Shape c e u -> streamMarksTo limit off u
        Fermata c -> Nil
        Note x -> Nil
        Play e -> Nil
        Assigned v -> Nil
        Rest -> Nil
        Seq a b -> streamMarksTo limit off a
                ++ streamMarksTo limit (off + durOf a) b
        Par a b -> mergeMarks (streamMarksTo limit off a)
                              (streamMarksTo limit off b)
        At n t -> streamMarksTo limit (off + n) t
        Scale t k -> placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
            (a, m) -> (a * k, m))
            (streamMarksTo (prim_div_int (limit - off) k) 0 t))
        Shrink t k -> placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
            (a, m) -> (prim_div_int a k, m))
            (streamMarksTo ((limit - off) * k) 0 t))
        Retro t -> reverse (placeMarks off (map (e => case e of
            (a, m) -> (durOf t - a, m)) (streamMarksTo (durOf t) 0 t)))
        Sow n t -> streamMarksTo limit off (sowScore n t)
        Clip w t -> placeMarks off
                        (streamMarksTo (minInt w (limit - off)) 0 t)

# ── The unfolding half — `spec/dynamicscore.md`, stage two ───────────────
#
# `layVoicesOnto` above walks the whole layout before its first event
# exists: an accumulator, reversed once at the end — the right shape for
# a score that ends, and no shape at all for one that does not.  What
# follows is the same arithmetic emitted the other way round: an event
# stream in **beat order**, lazy in its tail, so a performer can force
# enough to cover the next few beats and no more.
#
# Beat order is what the merge buys.  An overlay's two branches may both
# be endless, and flattening depth-first would play the first for ever;
# `mergeVoices` takes whichever branch sounds next, so two endless voices
# interleave.  A *sequence* appends instead — its right half cannot sound
# before its left half ends, so an endless left half never yielding it is
# what `forever ++ coda` means.
#
# Two spellings of where-a-note-is, held together the way the engines
# are: for every score that ends, this stream sounds exactly what
# `layVoices` lays out (`test_lazyscore.py`, change for change through
# the allocator).  `at` with a negative offset can locally disorder a
# seam — an event may arrive after one that sounds later — and that is
# the performer's to absorb or refuse: it reorders inside its horizon
# window and drops, with a report, what falls outside.

#: A score's **duration in ticks, from its shape alone** — no events are
#: laid to answer it, which is what lets a sequence stream: the right
#: half's offset needs the left half's duration, not its notes.
#: The algebra is `layVoicesOnto`'s, separated from the laying: `at`
#: translates extent and leaves duration alone, an overlay lasts as long
#: as its longer branch, and every leaf — even a rest — is one beat.
durOf : [: a :] -> Int
durOf s = case s of
    Note x -> ticksPerBeat
    Play e -> ticksPerBeat
    Assigned v -> ticksPerBeat
    Rest -> ticksPerBeat
    Seq a b -> durOf a + durOf b
    Par a b -> maxInt (durOf a) (durOf b)
    At n t -> durOf t
    Scale t k -> durOf t * k
    Shrink t k -> prim_div_int (durOf t) k
    Retro t -> durOf t
    # A decision is one beat before it is made — the invariant that keeps
    # this function total on a listening score.  A clip's duration is the
    # box, whatever the content did.
    Sow n t -> durOf t
    Clip w t -> w
    Mark n -> 0
    # Zero, and the honest reading of it: durations are measured up to
    # the first unanswered leaf.  A sown tree has no `Draw` left, and a
    # `Hear`'s time is the answer's — the frontier's fact, made the
    # measure's (`spec/ariadne.md`, time linearized at the joints).
    Draw g -> 0
    Hear p ky g -> 0
    TempoShape e u -> durOf u
    Shape c e u -> durOf u
    Fermata c -> 0

#: Two beat-ordered streams as one.  **Left-biased** on a shared onset,
#: which keeps an overlay's branches in writing order at the instant they
#: coincide — the same order the eager layout gives a stable sort, so the
#: allocator makes the same choices either way.
mergeVoices : List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
mergeVoices xs ys = case xs of
    Nil -> ys
    x :: xt -> case ys of
        Nil -> xs
        y :: yt -> case x of
            (a, b, v) -> case y of
                (c, d, w) -> case a <= c of
                    True -> x :: mergeVoices xt ys
                    False -> y :: mergeVoices xs yt

streamVoicesAt : Int -> [: a :] -> List (Int, Int, Voice)
streamVoicesAt off s = case s of
    Note x -> Nil
    Play e -> Nil
    Assigned v -> (off, off + ticksPerBeat, v) :: Nil
    Rest -> Nil
    Seq a b -> streamVoicesAt off a ++ streamVoicesAt (off + durOf a) b
    Par a b -> mergeVoices (streamVoicesAt off a) (streamVoicesAt off b)
    At n t -> streamVoicesAt (off + n) t
    # Lazy where the eager walk was strict: `map` yields as its argument
    # does, so a scaled endless section still streams.
    Scale t k -> placeScaledV off k (streamVoicesAt 0 t)
    Shrink t k -> placeShrunkV off k (streamVoicesAt 0 t)
    # Strict by nature: a retrograde's first event is its subject's last,
    # so the subtree must end to be reversed.  An endless one diverges in
    # `durOf` first, which the performer's budget reports as a stall.
    Retro t -> reverse (placeRetroV off (durOf t) (streamVoicesAt 0 t))
    Sow n t -> streamVoicesAt off (sowScore n t)
    Mark n -> Nil                       # a point is not a note
    # The flat stream cannot await a reading; `liveVoices` below is the
    # walk that can, and a probing score is performed through it.
    Draw g -> Nil
    Hear p ky g -> Nil
    TempoShape e u -> streamVoicesAt off u
    Shape c e u -> streamVoicesAt off u
    Fermata c -> Nil
    # Lazily clipped: the stream is beat-ordered, so the box is a
    # `takeBelowV` — which is also what lets a `sown` continuation be
    # endless (`cycle` inside a one-beat decision) and still stream.
    Clip w t -> placeFromV off (takeBelowV w (streamVoicesAt 0 t))

# ── The seed arithmetic — SplitMix64, spelled portable ───────────────────
#
# Every operation is written against an explicit 2^64, so the G-machine's
# unbounded integers, an eager bake, and a Rust cursor with `u64` compute
# the **same bits** — `python draws == rust draws` is a parity test
# waiting to be written, and these constants are its contract:
#
#     golden   0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 = 11400714819323198485
#     2*golden 0x3C6EF372FE94F82A =  4354685564936845354  (mod 2^64)
#     mixB     0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9 = 13787848793156543929
#     mixD     0x94D049BB133111EB = 10723151780598845931
#
# `xorBits` folds the exclusive-or a bit at a time — the language has no
# bitwise primitive, and does not need one: draws happen at decision
# instants, a few per beat, and a hundred-odd reductions per draw is
# nothing beside forcing the score that asked.

wrap64 : Int -> Int
wrap64 x = prim_mod_int x 18446744073709551616

#: The primitive, because the fold below cost 64 case-splits per call
#: and a resume descends through two mix64s *per spine level* — 71
#: seconds of rejoin were measured before this line.  `xorGo` stays as
#: the primitive's written-out meaning: what `prim_xor_int` computes,
#: what `crust`'s `XorInt` is held to, and what either would be checked
#: against if they ever disagreed.
xorBits : Int -> Int -> Int
xorBits a b = prim_xor_int a b

xorGo : Int -> Int -> Int -> Int -> Int
xorGo a b bit acc = case a + b of
    0 -> acc
    _ -> xorGo (prim_div_int a 2) (prim_div_int b 2) (bit * 2)
               (acc + bit * prim_mod_int (a + b) 2)

#: `z ^ (z >> 30)`, and its two siblings — shifts are division, because
#: the numbers are kept non-negative by `wrap64` at every entry.
mixA : Int -> Int
mixA z = xorBits z (prim_div_int z 1073741824)

#: `(a * b) mod 2^64`, with every intermediate under 2^96.  A full
#: 64×64 product reaches 2^128 − 2^65 — one bit past what a signed
#: 128-bit machine holds — and `crust` refuses it rather than wrap;
#: the reference never noticed, Python's integers having no width,
#: which is exactly the kind of fact a second machine exists to
#: surface.  Split the left factor at 2^32 and reduce the high half
#: early: bit-identical mod 2^64, so no draw moves.
mulWrap : Int -> Int -> Int
mulWrap a b =
    wrap64 (prim_mod_int a 4294967296 * b
            + prim_mod_int (prim_div_int a 4294967296 * b) 4294967296
              * 4294967296)

mixB : Int -> Int
mixB z = mulWrap z 13787848793156543929

mixC : Int -> Int
mixC z = xorBits z (prim_div_int z 134217728)

mixD : Int -> Int
mixD z = mulWrap z 10723151780598845931

mixE : Int -> Int
mixE z = xorBits z (prim_div_int z 2147483648)

mix64 : Int -> Int
mix64 z = mixE (mixD (mixC (mixB (mixA (wrap64 z)))))

#: One uniform draw in 0.0 .. 1.0 — the top 53 bits, which is every bit
#: a `Float` can hold.
unit : Int -> Float
unit s = toFloat (prim_div_int (mix64 (wrap64 s)) 2048) / 9007199254740992.0


# ── The live walk — a stream that can end in a question ──────────────────
#
# Ariadne's interpreter (`spec/ariadne.md`).  Every stream is
# **self-terminated**: it ends with `CueEnd e`, the tick its subtree
# actually reached — answers included — so `Seq` is `spliceEnd`
# (continue where the left *really* ended) and `durOf` appears nowhere
# in this walk.  That is what lets a `hear`'s content take exactly the
# time it takes with no wrapper accumulating per answer: the first cut
# shifted the continuation's tail once per question, which piled a
# `placeCues` layer per answer and ground the editor to a halt on a
# quarter-beat listener — measured, and the reason this shape exists.
# The lemmas each push bounds and context *into* a question's
# continuation (`streamMarksTo`'s lesson, still), and each now carries
# the end through its own arithmetic.
#
# On a question-free tree this walk must sound exactly
# `streamVoicesAt` — the parity the suites hold, change for change.

Cue := CueEv Int Int Voice
     | CueAsk Int (Chan (List Int)) Int (List Int -> List Cue)
     | CueEnd Int
     | CueHold Int (Chan Int)

#: Continue a self-terminated stream: walk to its `CueEnd`, hand the
#: end tick to what follows.  This is `Seq` — and it is why `durOf`
#: no longer appears in the live walk at all: **the width of a
#: subtree is a fact its own stream reports**, answers included,
#: which is what makes a `hear`'s content take exactly the time it
#: takes (`spec/ariadne.md`, time linearized at the joints).
spliceEnd : List Cue -> (Int -> List Cue) -> List Cue
spliceEnd cs f = case cs of
    Nil -> Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v -> c :: spliceEnd ct f
        CueAsk tk p ky k -> CueAsk tk p ky (zs => spliceEnd (k zs) f) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch -> c :: spliceEnd ct f
        CueEnd e -> f e

#: The larger of a finished branch's end and whatever the surviving
#: branch reports — `Par`'s join, where the constant law demands the
#: overlay be as wide as its widest answered branch.
raiseEnd : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
raiseEnd e cs = case cs of
    Nil -> CueEnd e :: Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v -> c :: raiseEnd e ct
        CueAsk tk p ky k -> CueAsk tk p ky (zs => raiseEnd e (k zs)) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch -> c :: raiseEnd e ct
        CueEnd e2 -> CueEnd (maxInt e e2) :: Nil

#: A declared box's own end, whatever the content did — `Clip`'s cap.
endAt : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
endAt w cs = case cs of
    Nil -> CueEnd w :: Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v -> c :: endAt w ct
        CueAsk tk p ky k -> CueAsk tk p ky (zs => endAt w (k zs)) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch -> c :: endAt w ct
        CueEnd e -> CueEnd w :: Nil

mergeCues : List Cue -> List Cue -> List Cue
mergeCues xs ys = case xs of
    Nil -> ys
    x :: xt -> case x of
        CueEnd ea -> raiseEnd ea ys
        _ -> case ys of
            Nil -> xs
            y :: yt -> case y of
                CueEnd eb -> raiseEnd eb xs
                _ -> case cueTick x <= cueTick y of
                    True -> case x of
                        CueEv a b v -> x :: mergeCues xt ys
                        CueAsk tk p ky k ->
                            CueAsk tk p ky (zs => mergeCues (k zs) ys) :: Nil
                        CueHold tk ch -> x :: mergeCues xt ys
                        CueEnd e -> raiseEnd e ys
                    False -> case y of
                        CueEv a b v -> y :: mergeCues xs yt
                        CueAsk tk p ky k ->
                            CueAsk tk p ky (zs => mergeCues xs (k zs)) :: Nil
                        CueHold tk ch -> y :: mergeCues xs yt
                        CueEnd e -> raiseEnd e xs

cueTick : Cue -> Int
cueTick c = case c of
    CueEv a b v -> a
    CueAsk tk p ky k -> tk
    CueHold tk ch -> tk
    CueEnd e -> e

cueBelow : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
cueBelow w cs = case cs of
    Nil -> Nil
    c :: ct -> case cueTick c < w of
        False -> Nil
        True -> case c of
            CueEv a b v -> c :: cueBelow w ct
            CueAsk tk p ky k -> CueAsk tk p ky (zs => cueBelow w (k zs)) :: Nil
            CueHold tk ch -> c :: cueBelow w ct
            CueEnd e -> CueEnd e :: Nil

placeCues : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
placeCues off cs = case cs of
    Nil -> Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v -> CueEv (off + a) (off + b) v :: placeCues off ct
        CueAsk tk p ky k ->
            CueAsk (off + tk) p ky (zs => placeCues off (k zs)) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch -> CueHold (off + tk) ch :: placeCues off ct
        CueEnd e -> CueEnd (off + e) :: Nil

scaleCues : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
scaleCues k cs = case cs of
    Nil -> Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v -> CueEv (a * k) (b * k) v :: scaleCues k ct
        CueAsk tk p ky q ->
            CueAsk (tk * k) p ky (zs => scaleCues k (q zs)) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch -> CueHold (tk * k) ch :: scaleCues k ct
        CueEnd e -> CueEnd (e * k) :: Nil

shrinkCues : Int -> List Cue -> List Cue
shrinkCues k cs = case cs of
    Nil -> Nil
    c :: ct -> case c of
        CueEv a b v ->
            CueEv (prim_div_int a k) (prim_div_int b k) v :: shrinkCues k ct
        CueAsk tk p ky q ->
            CueAsk (prim_div_int tk k) p ky
                (zs => shrinkCues k (q zs)) :: Nil
        CueHold tk ch ->
            CueHold (prim_div_int tk k) ch :: shrinkCues k ct
        CueEnd e -> CueEnd (prim_div_int e k) :: Nil

cuesOfEvents : List (Int, Int, Voice) -> List Cue -> List Cue
cuesOfEvents es rest = case es of
    Nil -> rest
    e :: et -> case e of
        (a, b, v) -> CueEv a b v :: cuesOfEvents et rest

liveTo : Int -> [: a :] -> List Cue
liveTo off s = case s of
    Assigned v -> CueEv off (off + ticksPerBeat) v
               :: CueEnd (off + ticksPerBeat) :: Nil
    Note x -> CueEnd (off + ticksPerBeat) :: Nil
    Play e -> CueEnd (off + ticksPerBeat) :: Nil
    Rest -> CueEnd (off + ticksPerBeat) :: Nil
    Mark n -> CueEnd off :: Nil
    Seq a b -> spliceEnd (liveTo off a) (e => liveTo e b)
    Par a b -> mergeCues (liveTo off a) (liveTo off b)
    # `at` moves the extent and leaves the duration alone: the content
    # walks shifted, the reported end does not move with it.
    At n t -> spliceEnd (liveTo (off + n) t) (e => CueEnd (e - n) :: Nil)
    Scale t k -> placeCues off (scaleCues k (liveTo 0 t))
    Shrink t k -> placeCues off (shrinkCues k (liveTo 0 t))
    # A retrograde cannot listen: its first event is its subject's last,
    # so a question inside it has no answer that is not also the past.
    # Questions under `reverse` are silent, deliberately and documented.
    Retro t -> cuesOfEvents (reverse (placeRetroV off (durOf t)
                                      (streamVoicesAt 0 t)))
                            (CueEnd (off + durOf t) :: Nil)
    Sow n t -> liveTo off (sowScore n t)
    Clip w t -> placeCues off (endAt w (cueBelow w (liveTo 0 t)))
    Draw g -> CueEnd off :: Nil
    # Ariadne's question, unboxed and shift-free: the answer's own walk
    # reports where it ended, and `spliceEnd` one level up places what
    # follows there — the width of a decision is a fact its stream
    # tells, never a number a wrapper adds.
    Hear p ky g -> CueAsk off p ky (zs => liveTo off (g zs)) :: Nil
    TempoShape e u -> liveTo off u
    Shape c e u -> liveTo off u
    # A fermata is a point the clock waits at: the cue says where
    # and on which channel, and the walk carries straight on — the
    # waiting is the performer's, not the layout's.
    Fermata c -> CueHold off c :: CueEnd off :: Nil
